            <div xml:id="d526">

                <pb facs="0722" n="632" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg632"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">300.115/20523: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-10T16:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 10,
                                1919—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 10—3:22
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>3510. In reply to Department’s 5885, August 28, 5 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >3</hi>] p.m., contents of which I transmitted to the British Government on
                    September 2d, Foreign Office has just addressed to me following note:<anchor
                        n="35" xml:id="d526-f1"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I have now the honor to inform Your Excellency that His Majesty’s
                            Government will be quite prepared to consider, and in proper cases to
                            consent to, an order for the release upon such terms as the court may
                            deem just of goods which were detained under the Order in Council of
                            March 11, 1915, to American claimants who may establish their ownership
                            at the time of shipment. The proceeds of goods already sold or the
                            invoice values deposited to which Your Excellency refers, would be dealt
                            with in like manner. As observed in my former note of August 24th, 1918,
                            it would be understood that such releases are accepted in final
                            settlement of all claims. For this purpose I would suggest that claims
                            accompanied by documentary proof of such ownership should be forwarded
                            by the claimants to His Majesty’s Procurator General direct.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>For Foreign Office’s note of August 24th, 1918, see my number 9821, of September
                    3d, 1918<anchor n="36" xml:id="d526-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
